messages:
fran: Heads-up: found drift in the mobile/samples README. The .NET sample in the mobile repo still has a hardcoded API key and uses synchronous blocking calls. Someone copied it into an app and hit deadlocks on the UI thread. Filed SUP-4768.

fran: snippet shows:

```fran: // Bad example - DO NOT COPY INTO UI CODE
var client = new HttpClient();
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", "sk-EXAMPLEKEY");
var res = client.PostAsync("https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate", new StringContent(json)).Result; // blocking .Result on UI
Console.WriteLine(res.StatusCode);
```

tom: oh no. We also bumped the .NET SDK to async-only in v1.2 last month; the sample still shows the old sync-based wrapper. That's the drift.

harper: plan: replace hardcoded key with secure storage / env var pattern, switch to async Task usage with CancellationToken, and show how to use HttpClientFactory or the SDK. Also call out that SDK auto-retries 429/5xx with jittered expo backoff; raw HttpClient needs explicit retry policy (Polly).

fran: Proposed safe sample (one-shot HTTP) and preferred SDK variant:

```fran: // Safe raw HTTP (one-off, non-UI)
var apiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REDWOOD_API_KEY");
using var http = new HttpClient { Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10) };
http.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", apiKey);
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
var res = await http.PostAsync("https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate", new StringContent(json), cts.Token);
res.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
```

```fran: // Preferred: SDK (handles pooling + retries). Async-only v1.2+
var client = new Redwood.Client(new Redwood.Configuration { ApiKey = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("REDWOOD_API_KEY") });
// SDK auto-retries 429/5xx by default; to opt out set RetryPolicy = null
var resp = await client.GenerateAsync(new GenerateRequest { Prompt = "hi" }, cancellationToken);
```

tom: Also add a short note in the README: "If you copy snippets into UI code, use async/await and CancellationToken; do not use .Result or .GetAwaiter().GetResult()". That would prevent the deadlock footgun.

harper: CI idea: add a docs grep that fails on patterns like `sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{6,}` or `REPLACE_ME` in markdown and .csx/.cs examples. Also detect `.Result` or `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` in docs flagged as "UI sample" files. Simple grep in docs pipeline.

ci-bot: Proposed grep checks: `grep -E "sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{6,}|REPLACE_ME" docs/ samples/ || exit 1` and `grep -En "\.(Result|getAwaiter\(\)\.GetResult\()" samples/mobile || true` (warn-level).

fran: One more parity note: update the top-level SDK compatibility table in quickstart to list the .NET package name and the minimum async-only version so people don't copy the older sync patterns.

docs-bot: New PR opened by harper: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/721  — title: "mobile/dotnet: remove hardcoded key, update async samples, add docs-lint"

tom: I'll review. After merge, security/support should rotate the leaked key and we should add an author checklist entry: "never paste tokens in examples; prefer env var or secure storage."

harper: thanks — landing today. :white_check_mark:
